WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.394
Julius Caesar wanted to send
orders that nobody else could read.

2
00:00:08.614 --> 00:00:13.862
So he hid them in a cipher: a rule
that turns a message into secret code.

3
00:00:14.362 --> 00:00:19.006
Caesar moved every letter the same
number of places along the alphabet.

4
00:00:19.226 --> 00:00:23.823
That number is the key. With
a shift of three, a becomes d.

5
00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:29.105
So cat becomes f d w. Each letter
has moved three places along.

6
00:00:29.325 --> 00:00:34.898
At the end of the alphabet you wrap
round to the start. y shifted three is b.

7
00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:40.366
To read it, shift back by the
same number. f d w says cat again.

8
00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:45.045
Pigpen does not shift letters.
It swaps each one for a shape.

9
00:00:45.265 --> 00:00:50.931
Draw a grid like noughts and
crosses. Put a to i in its nine pens.

10
00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:54.541
A letter's symbol is the shape
of the lines round its pen.

11
00:00:54.761 --> 00:01:00.519
a is in the top-left pen, so a is
a corner, open at the top and left.

12
00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:05.337
e sits in the middle pen, with lines
all round, so e is a closed box.

13
00:01:05.557 --> 00:01:12.569
A second grid holds j to r, each pen
with a dot. Two X shapes hold s to z.

14
00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:17.619
Write cat in Pigpen and you get
three shapes. Read them back: cat.

15
00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:23.785
A cipher is only as secret as its
key. Whoever holds the key reads it.

16
00:01:24.005 --> 00:01:27.488
Caesar had to tell his
generals the shift in advance.

17
00:01:27.708 --> 00:01:32.166
If a messenger carrying the key
was caught, every message was open.

18
00:01:32.386 --> 00:01:35.869
A Caesar cipher has only
twenty-five shifts to try.

19
00:01:36.089 --> 00:01:41.801
Try them one by one until words appear.
It is a good puzzle and a weak lock.

20
00:01:42.301 --> 00:01:45.970
Your browser and your messages
use the very same idea.

21
00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.719
Scramble the message with a
key. Unscramble it with the key.

22
00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:53.794
The difference is the key.
Today's keys are enormously long.

23
00:01:54.014 --> 00:01:58.054
Trying every one would take longer
than the age of the universe.

24
00:01:58.274 --> 00:02:02.036
That is why your password and your
card number are safe on the way.

25
00:02:02.536 --> 00:02:06.808
Every cipher makes the same
journey, and each stage has a name.

26
00:02:07.028 --> 00:02:11.533
You start with plain text: the
message anybody could read.

27
00:02:11.753 --> 00:02:15.375
You encrypt it with the key,
and out comes ciphertext.

28
00:02:15.595 --> 00:02:19.496
It travels. At the other
end the key decrypts it.

29
00:02:19.716 --> 00:02:24.128
The plain text is back. Caesar
and Pigpen both make that trip.

30
00:02:24.628 --> 00:02:28.157
Caesar shifts every letter
along the alphabet by the key.

31
00:02:28.377 --> 00:02:31.860
Pigpen gives every letter the
shape of the pen it sits in.

32
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.724
The key is the secret, and
twenty-five shifts is a weak secret.

33
00:02:36.944 --> 00:02:40.938
Plain text in, ciphertext out,
and the key brings it back.
